/* Notes for s13 — generated by tools/build-notes.js from content/notes. Do not edit by hand. */
PG.notes("l074", "<p class=\"summary\">Brief for Capstone Project 2: build a personal portfolio site using only HTML and CSS. There is no solution walkthrough because capstones are meant to be the learner's own work.</p>\n<h3>Capstone project ground rules</h3>\n<ul><li>Capstones have no solution code, no video walkthrough and no design spec, so the finished site can be presented as original work in a portfolio.</li><li>Getting stuck is the point: gaps in memory show which earlier lessons or docs to revisit.</li><li>Deliverables are one HTML file, one CSS file and any images used. No JavaScript is allowed in this project.</li><li>A live example site exists at appbrewery.github.io/capstone-2-example for visual inspiration.</li></ul>\n<h3>Required features</h3>\n<ul><li>Hero section giving a short introduction to you and your background.</li><li>Resume page or section listing education, work experience, skills and other details (may link to Capstone 1).</li><li>Projects section showing past work.</li><li>About-me section covering interests.</li><li>Contact section with ways to get in touch.</li></ul>\n<h3>Technical requirements</h3>\n<ul><li>Valid HTML using semantic elements (header, nav, main, section, footer).</li><li>Valid CSS with sensible naming and organisation.</li><li>Responsive layout that works across screen sizes and devices.</li><li>Every image needs meaningful alt text.</li></ul>\n<h3>Suggested five-hour timeline</h3>\n<ul><li>Hour 1: collect content and design ideas, sketch wireframes and mockups.</li><li>Hours 2-3: write the HTML structure and CSS, testing in different browsers and devices.</li><li>Hour 4: finalise design and copy, revise, optimise performance.</li><li>Hour 5: launch, share in the Q&amp;A for feedback, make final tweaks.</li></ul>\n<h2>The task: Capstone 2: Personal portfolio site</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Empty project; you create index.html, styles.css and an images folder.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> A responsive, semantic, accessible, HTML+CSS-only personal website with hero, resume, projects, about and contact sections.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Plan content and sketch a wireframe of each section.</li><li>Write semantic HTML for the hero, resume, projects, about and contact sections.</li><li>Style with an external stylesheet; make it responsive with media queries or flexible layouts.</li><li>Add alt text to every image.</li><li>Validate HTML and CSS, test on several screen sizes, then share for feedback.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A polished portfolio page with all five sections and no JavaScript.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo copy includes a script.js with theme toggle and mobile menu code, which breaks the brief's no-JavaScript rule; also it contains the student's personal name and email, which should be ignored.</li><li>Forgetting alt text or a viewport meta tag, which hurt accessibility and responsiveness.</li></ul>");
